  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Contested Legacies is already showing early scholarly and practical relevance for debates on housing, urban transformation, and sustainable cities. The book has attracted 2 citations, 1,230 OAPEN downloads, and aligns with the SDG theme of Sustainable cities and communities, suggesting that it is being read as both a research contribution and a resource for policy-oriented discussions.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2023, Contested Legacies addresses postwar housing as a historical, social, and ideological problem rather than a purely technical one. Its focus on the mismatch between built environments and the narratives attached to them gives the book clear relevance to architecture, urban studies, and the politics of housing, especially in the context of current housing and environmental pressures.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The usage record points to active readership: 1,230 downloads through OAPEN indicate that the book has been accessed well beyond a narrow specialist audience. That pattern is consistent with a work that is useful to readers working across architecture, planning, and related social science fields, even though the citation base is still modest.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is early but meaningful. Two 2025 journal articles in Europe Asia Studies and Eurasian Geography and Economics have cited the book, both in the context of urbanization and city planning, with additional overlap in discussions of housing, finance, neoliberalism, and urban development and cultural heritage. This shows that the book is beginning to inform scholarly conversations about large housing estates and socialist-era mass housing.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals reinforce its broader relevance. The book is available as open access in a hybrid format, has a teaching and holdings trace in WorldCat, and carries funding support from KU Leuven. Taken together, the record suggests a book with growing academic uptake, practical sustainability relevance, and a readership that extends into policy-adjacent and interdisciplinary housing debates.</p>",
